Please note that the last condition in your assertions (containment check) did not match for the method that tested that assert condition, so I omitted that one because the existing condition was:

```cs
[Test]
public void Test_IsTrue_ContainsTest()
{
    Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX); //If the return value is false, state of x's presence in the list does not remain the same.
}
```

But the condition that I wanted to assert here should be to compare `Old_arrListContainsX` and `New_arrListContainsX`, not the opposite way. I've decided not to include that condition because the behaviour of checking if the value exists in the Array does not increase (like it should in a `PUT_AddContract` test method for example) -- the check would not show any difference if the value already exists so it would be the same after adding the value. You may want to revise this.--------------------```cs
// @@@ Old and new counts should be equal in CountContract
Debug.Assert(arrList.Count == New_Ret);

// @@@ When Add is called, the count should be increased by 1
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount + 1 == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ After Add, the new list should have the newly added item at the last position
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == true);

// @@@ Before Remove, the list should have the item
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX == true);

// @@@ After Remove, the new list should not have the removed item
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == false);

// @@@ If the return value of IndexOf is -1, then the item does not exist in the collection
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX == -1 || New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);

// @@@ If return value of LastIndexOf is index, then the item exists in the collection
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == index || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == index);

// @@@ If the return value is false, last position of x in new list is exactly -1
Debug.Assert(arrList.Contains(x) == false || New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
```
